WEBVTT

00:00:00.202 --> 00:00:02.698
The diaphragm contracts
and becomes flatter.

00:00:03.164 --> 00:00:07.702
The external intercostal muscles
contract, moving the ribs up and out.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:12.361
Chest volume increases, the lungs
expand, and air pressure in them falls

00:00:12.372 --> 00:00:14.358
briefly below atmospheric pressure.

00:00:15.135 --> 00:00:15.742
Contract

00:00:15.742 --> 00:00:18.601
means the diaphragm gets
flatter, not more domed.

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:21.250
That feels backwards
until I see the drawing.

00:00:22.041 --> 00:00:23.625
Now breathe out quietly.

00:00:24.382 --> 00:00:26.855
The diaphragm relaxes and curves upward.

00:00:27.303 --> 00:00:29.536
The ribcage moves down and in.

00:00:30.046 --> 00:00:32.584
Elastic recoil helps reduce lung volume.

00:00:33.021 --> 00:00:37.272
Air pressure rises briefly above
outside pressure, so air flows out.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.352
Quiet breathing out is mostly passive.

00:00:40.816 --> 00:00:42.637
I don't need to squeeze
with all my strength.

00:00:43.314 --> 00:00:43.801
Right.

00:00:44.269 --> 00:00:48.550
Forced breathing, such as blowing
hard, uses extra muscle effort.

00:00:48.943 --> 00:00:51.748
Our simple diagram
describes quiet breathing.

00:00:52.142 --> 00:00:53.856
The bottle model has limits too.

00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:59.517
Its rigid sides cannot show ribs moving,
and its sheet is not a real domed muscle.

00:01:00.227 --> 00:01:05.608
Exam answer: diaphragm contracts,
ribs up and out, volume increases,

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.180
pressure decreases, air enters.

00:01:08.575 --> 00:01:09.443
Five links.

00:01:09.673 --> 00:01:11.163
No invisible lung pump.

00:01:11.854 --> 00:01:12.557
Excellent.

00:01:13.029 --> 00:01:16.428
If a question asks why air
enters, pressure lower than

00:01:16.428 --> 00:01:18.432
outside is the vital explanation.

00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:22.826
A model is useful when we explain both
what it shows and what it leaves out.

00:01:23.202 --> 00:01:25.277
We have delivered air to an alveolus.

00:01:26.109 --> 00:01:29.820
This red loop around it is a
capillary, a tiny blood vessel.

00:01:30.305 --> 00:01:32.462
Does the whole air bubble
jump into the blood?

00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:33.498
No.

00:01:33.994 --> 00:01:36.416
Individual gases cross the thin wall.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.543
An air bubble in a blood
vessel is not the plan.

00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:40.888
Correct.

00:01:41.286 --> 00:01:44.245
Oxygen diffuses from
alveolar air into the blood.

00:01:44.694 --> 00:01:48.347
Carbon dioxide diffuses from
blood into the alveolus, ready

00:01:48.348 --> 00:01:49.920
to leave during exhalation.

00:01:50.815 --> 00:01:54.006
The oxygen knows where the muscles
are and chooses the right direction?

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.452
No tiny navigation system.

00:01:56.831 --> 00:01:58.359
Particles move randomly.

00:01:58.624 --> 00:02:02.559
The net movement is down a gradient
from where that gas is more available

00:02:02.574 --> 00:02:03.993
to where it is less available.

00:02:04.522 --> 00:02:07.980
At this level, we describe it as
higher to lower concentration.

00:02:08.739 --> 00:02:13.169
Blood returning from body tissues has
less oxygen and more carbon dioxide

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.327
because the cells have been respiring.

00:02:16.122 --> 00:02:16.913
Exactly.

00:02:17.314 --> 00:02:20.664
Fresh alveolar air and
arriving blood create opposite

00:02:20.666 --> 00:02:22.289
gradients for the two gases.

00:02:22.628 --> 00:02:24.640
They cross in opposite directions.

00:02:25.296 --> 00:02:29.709
Oxygen attaches to hemoglobin in red
blood cells for transport around the body.

00:02:30.339 --> 00:02:34.565
So the heart pumps blood, but it doesn't
pump oxygen through the alveolar wall.

00:02:35.348 --> 00:02:36.077
Well put.

00:02:36.435 --> 00:02:39.050
Gas exchange across
that wall is diffusion.

00:02:39.369 --> 00:02:42.946
Blood flow carries gases to
and from the exchange surface.

00:02:43.482 --> 00:02:45.811
Two different mechanisms working together.

00:02:46.612 --> 00:02:50.274
What would happen if the same
air and blood just sat there?

00:02:51.085 --> 00:02:54.752
The differences would become smaller
and net diffusion would slow.

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:57.848
Ventilation refreshes the alveolar air.

00:02:58.419 --> 00:03:02.386
Circulation brings blood needing
oxygen and carries oxygenated blood

00:03:02.387 --> 00:03:04.571
away, maintaining the gradients.

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:08.840
Refresh the air, move the
blood, keep the difference.

00:03:09.223 --> 00:03:10.625
The crossing stays busy.

00:03:11.372 --> 00:03:12.211
Exactly.

00:03:12.545 --> 00:03:15.488
The cartoon particles
are enlarged enormously.

00:03:15.842 --> 00:03:19.801
Real molecules do not wait in neat
queues or cross one at a time.

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:24.503
Our arrows show the net movement,
not every particle's individual path

00:03:24.847 --> 00:03:26.233
You are designing lungs.

00:03:26.742 --> 00:03:30.384
Would one enormous smooth airbag
or millions of small air sacs

00:03:30.581 --> 00:03:32.395
give more surface for exchange?

00:03:33.164 --> 00:03:35.013
Millions of small sacs.

00:03:35.552 --> 00:03:39.362
Like cutting a big block into
smaller pieces exposes more surface.

00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:40.558
Good.

00:03:40.915 --> 00:03:46.255
Many alveoli create a large total surface
area, so more gas can cross at once.

00:03:46.653 --> 00:03:50.476
Now choose a wall: thick castle
wall or very thin barrier.

00:03:51.287 --> 00:03:52.121
Thin barrier.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.369
A short diffusion distance
means faster exchange.

00:03:56.063 --> 00:04:00.305
Each alveolar wall and nearby
capillary wall is only one cell thick.

00:04:00.722 --> 00:04:03.879
The lining is moist, so gases
can dissolve before crossing.

00:04:04.359 --> 00:04:08.024
A rich capillary supply and
continuous ventilation keep the

00:04:08.029 --> 00:04:09.683
concentration gradient steep.

00:04:10.456 --> 00:04:15.390
Large surface, thin moist walls,
good blood supply, and fresh air.

00:04:16.076 --> 00:04:17.563
Five design features.

00:04:18.218 --> 00:04:20.200
Don't just list them
in an explain question.

00:04:20.561 --> 00:04:22.339
Link each feature to its job.

00:04:22.658 --> 00:04:25.062
Thin walls mean a short
diffusion distance.

00:04:25.417 --> 00:04:28.513
Many alveoli mean more
surface for gases to cross.

00:04:28.784 --> 00:04:30.792
Blood flow helps maintain the gradient.

00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:36.909
So writing alveoli are good because they
are good is not my award-winning answer.

00:04:37.706 --> 00:04:40.000
It would make my imaginary
marking pen sigh.
